# Airspace and airport operations: 14 CFR 91.126 to 91.135, 91.155, 91.215, 91.225; AIM chapters 2 to 4 and 7-4 (wake turbulence). ACS areas I.E and III.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 1, "Class B airspace", "What is required before a VFR pilot may enter Class B airspace?",
      ["A clearance from ATC to enter it",
       "Two-way radio contact with the approach controller",
       "An instrument rating and an IFR flight plan on file"],
      "14 CFR 91.131 requires an ATC clearance to operate in Class B airspace. Two-way radio contact is enough for Class C and D. VFR flight is allowed in Class B with a clearance."),
    S(1, 2, "VFR weather minimums", "What are the basic VFR minimums in Class C airspace?",
      ["3 statute miles visibility, 500 feet below, 1,000 feet above and 2,000 feet horizontally from clouds",
       "3 statute miles visibility and clear of clouds, with no other cloud separation needed",
       "1 statute mile visibility, 500 feet below, 1,000 feet above and 2,000 feet horizontally from clouds"],
      "14 CFR 91.155 gives Class C and D the same minimums as Class E below 10,000 feet MSL: 3 statute miles, 500 below, 1,000 above, 2,000 horizontal. Clear of clouds with 3 miles is the Class B rule."),
    S(1, 2, "Class G minimums", "In Class G airspace, during the day, at 1,000 feet AGL, what are the VFR minimums?",
      ["1 statute mile visibility and clear of clouds",
       "3 statute miles visibility and clear of clouds",
       "1 statute mile visibility, 500 feet below and 1,000 feet above clouds"],
      "At or below 1,200 feet above the surface in Class G during the day, 14 CFR 91.155 requires 1 statute mile and clear of clouds. At night it rises to 3 statute miles with 500/1,000/2,000 cloud clearances."),
    S(1, 1, "Speed limits", "Unless authorized by ATC, what is the maximum indicated airspeed at or below 2,500 feet AGL within 4 nautical miles of the primary airport of a Class D area?",
      ["200 knots", "230 knots", "250 knots"],
      "14 CFR 91.117(b) limits speed to 200 knots at or below 2,500 feet AGL within 4 NM of the primary airport of Class C or D airspace. The 250-knot limit applies below 10,000 feet MSL generally."),
    S(1, 2, "Light gun signals", "While taxiing toward the runway with a failed radio, you see a flashing green light from the tower. What does it mean?",
      ["Cleared to taxi", "Cleared for takeoff", "Return to the starting point"],
      "On the ground, flashing green means cleared to taxi, steady green means cleared for takeoff, and flashing white means return to the starting point on the airport."),
    S(1, 2, "Airport signs", "A sign with white letters on a red background reading 18-36 is beside a taxiway. What must you do?",
      ["Hold short unless ATC has cleared you to enter or cross the runway",
       "Continue taxiing, because the sign only shows the runway direction",
       "Stop and wait for a light gun signal before crossing the marking"],
      "White on red is a mandatory instruction sign. A runway holding position sign marks the hold point for that runway; at a towered airport you need a clearance to cross it."),
    S(1, 3, "Wake turbulence", "You are landing behind a large jet that has just landed on the same runway. How should you plan your approach?",
      ["Stay at or above the jet's flight path and touch down beyond its touchdown point",
       "Stay below the jet's flight path and touch down before its touchdown point",
       "Fly the normal glidepath and touch down at the usual point, since the vortices always rise"],
      "Wake vortices sink and stop being generated when the nose wheel touches down. The AIM advises staying at or above the larger aircraft's path and landing beyond its touchdown point."),
    S(1, 1, "Transponder codes", "Which transponder code should a VFR pilot squawk to report an emergency?",
      ["7700", "7600", "7500"],
      "7700 is the general emergency code, 7600 is lost communications, 7500 is unlawful interference (hijack) and 1200 is the standard VFR code."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 2, "Class D communication", "You call a Class D tower and the controller replies, \"Aircraft calling Riverside Tower, standby.\" May you enter the Class D airspace?",
      ["No, because the controller did not use your call sign",
       "Yes, because the controller has answered your call",
       "Yes, as long as you stay above 1,500 feet AGL in the area"],
      "To enter Class D airspace two-way radio communication must be established (14 CFR 91.129). If the controller answers using your call sign, it is established; a reply without your call sign is not."),
    S(2, 2, "Class E minimums", "What are the VFR minimums in Class E airspace at or above 10,000 feet MSL?",
      ["5 statute miles, 1,000 feet below, 1,000 feet above and 1 statute mile horizontally from clouds",
       "3 statute miles, 500 feet below, 1,000 feet above and 2,000 feet horizontally from clouds",
       "5 statute miles, 500 feet below, 1,000 feet above and 2,000 feet horizontally from clouds"],
      "14 CFR 91.155: in Class E at or above 10,000 feet MSL, VFR requires 5 statute miles and 1,000 below, 1,000 above and 1 statute mile horizontal clearance from clouds."),
    S(2, 1, "Class A airspace", "Class A airspace extends from",
      ["18,000 feet MSL up to and including FL 600",
       "14,500 feet MSL up to but not including FL 180",
       "10,000 feet MSL up to and including FL 450"],
      "Class A airspace begins at 18,000 feet MSL and extends up to and including FL 600. All flight in Class A is IFR."),
    S(2, 3, "ADS-B and transponder", "Within how many nautical miles of a Class B primary airport must an aircraft generally have an operating Mode C transponder and ADS-B Out, from the surface to 10,000 feet MSL?",
      ["30 nautical miles", "20 nautical miles", "10 nautical miles"],
      "14 CFR 91.215 and 91.225 apply within the 30 NM Mode C veil around Class B primary airports, from the surface up to 10,000 feet MSL, with limited exceptions for aircraft certificated without an electrical system."),
    S(2, 2, "Runway markings", "On a runway, a series of yellow chevrons points toward the threshold from the area before it. How may that area be used?",
      ["It is not usable for taxi, takeoff or landing",
       "It may be used for takeoff but not for landing",
       "It may be used for taxi and for landing rollout"],
      "Yellow chevrons mark a blast pad or stopway that looks like runway but is not usable for taxi, takeoff or landing. A displaced threshold, marked with white arrows, can be used for taxi, takeoff and landing rollout."),
    S(2, 2, "Visual glide slope", "On approach, a PAPI shows two white lights and two red lights. What does this mean?",
      ["You are on the correct glidepath", "You are slightly above the glidepath", "You are below the glidepath"],
      "A PAPI shows two white and two red when on glidepath, more white when high (three white is slightly high, four white is high) and more red when low."),
    S(2, 1, "Airport beacon", "A rotating airport beacon flashes alternating white and green. What kind of airport is it?",
      ["A lighted civilian land airport", "A lighted heliport", "A lighted water airport"],
      "White and green identifies a lighted land airport, white and yellow a water airport, and green, yellow and white a heliport. A military airport also shows white and green, but with two quick white flashes between the green flashes."),
    S(2, 3, "Traffic pattern", "How should you enter the traffic pattern at a nontowered airport, according to the recommended procedure in the AIM?",
      ["At pattern altitude, on a 45-degree angle to the downwind leg abeam midfield",
       "On a long straight-in final while descending from cruise altitude",
       "Directly on the base leg after crossing over the top of the runway at midfield"],
      "The AIM's recommended entry is a 45-degree angle to the downwind leg, abeam the midpoint of the runway, at traffic pattern altitude. Standard pattern turns are to the left unless marked otherwise."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 1, "Class C airspace", "What is the usual vertical extent of the 5 nautical mile inner core of Class C airspace?",
      ["From the surface to 4,000 feet above the airport elevation",
       "From 1,200 feet AGL to 4,000 feet above the airport elevation",
       "From the surface to 10,000 feet MSL"],
      "Class C is typically a 5 NM core from the surface to 4,000 feet above the airport and a 10 NM shelf from 1,200 to 4,000 feet above the airport."),
    S(0, 2, "Special VFR", "What is needed for a special VFR clearance at night in a Class D surface area?",
      ["An instrument rating and an airplane equipped for IFR flight",
       "Only a private certificate and 1 mile of ground visibility",
       "A night endorsement and at least 3 miles of flight visibility"],
      "Special VFR requires 1 statute mile and clear of clouds. 14 CFR 91.157 adds that between sunset and sunrise the pilot must be instrument rated and the aircraft equipped for IFR."),
    S(0, 2, "Light gun signals", "You are in flight near a towered airport with a failed radio and see a steady red light. What does it mean?",
      ["Give way to other aircraft and continue circling",
       "Airport unsafe, do not land there under any circumstances",
       "Return for landing"],
      "In flight, steady red means give way and continue circling, flashing red means the airport is unsafe and not to land, and flashing green means return for landing."),
    S(0, 2, "Hold short markings", "A hold short line across a taxiway has two solid yellow lines and two dashed yellow lines. Which side do you stop on?",
      ["The side with the solid lines", "The side with the dashed lines", "On top of the solid lines"],
      "Approaching from the solid side, you must stop before the solid lines unless cleared. From the dashed side you may cross to exit the runway; you are clear of it once the whole aircraft is past the lines."),
    S(0, 1, "Airport signs", "What does a taxiway location sign look like?",
      ["Yellow letters on a black background with a yellow border",
       "Black letters on a yellow background with a black border",
       "White letters on a red background with a black outline"],
      "Location signs, which tell you where you are, are yellow on black. Direction and destination signs are black on yellow, and mandatory signs are white on red."),
    S(0, 2, "Special use airspace", "What should a VFR pilot do before flying through an active Military Operations Area?",
      ["Contact the controlling agency for traffic advisories and use extra caution",
       "Get a clearance from the controlling agency, which is required for every pilot",
       "Avoid the area entirely, since VFR flight through it is prohibited"],
      "VFR flight through an active MOA is allowed without a clearance, but the AIM advises contacting the controlling agency and exercising extreme caution. Restricted areas, unlike MOAs, need authorization when active."),
    S(0, 3, "Wake turbulence", "Which conditions produce the strongest wingtip vortices?",
      ["A heavy aircraft flying clean and slow",
       "A heavy aircraft flying with flaps and fast",
       "A light aircraft flying clean and fast"],
      "Vortex strength depends on weight, speed and wing shape. The greatest vortices come from a heavy, clean, slow aircraft, because it flies at a high angle of attack."),
    S(0, 2, "Wake turbulence", "Which wind condition can keep the wake of a landing large aircraft on the runway the longest?",
      ["A light quartering tailwind", "A strong direct headwind", "A strong direct crosswind of 20 knots"],
      "A light quartering tailwind can move the upwind vortex back onto the touchdown zone and keep it there. Strong winds break vortices up faster."),
    S(0, 1, "Common frequencies", "At a nontowered airport with no published frequency, which frequency do pilots use for self-announce calls?",
      ["122.9 MHz (MULTICOM)", "121.5 MHz (emergency)", "122.2 MHz (flight service)"],
      "With no tower, FSS or UNICOM, pilots self-announce on MULTICOM, 122.9 MHz. 121.5 MHz is the emergency frequency."),
    S(0, 3, "Class G night minimums", "At night in Class G airspace below 1,200 feet AGL, an airplane may fly with 1 mile visibility clear of clouds only when",
      ["it is in an airport traffic pattern within half a mile of the runway",
       "it is more than 10 miles from the nearest airport with a control tower",
       "it is flying with a filed and activated VFR flight plan"],
      "14 CFR 91.155(b)(2) allows 1 statute mile and clear of clouds at night in Class G below 1,200 feet AGL only in a traffic pattern within half a mile of the runway. Otherwise 3 miles and 500/1,000/2,000 apply."),
]
